[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Stay in your lane is two requirements, and the Crosses edge is the lane

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[MECHANISM]
UID: MECH-LANE-DISCIPLINE-IS-A-REQUIREMENT
TITLE: Stay in your lane is two requirements, and the Crosses edge is the lane
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATEMENT: >>>
The motivation behind SLICE is SCOPE DISCIPLINE, and it is two rules rather than
a type:

  If you are NOT working a slice, stay inside one horizontal layer.
  If you ARE working a slice, stay inside the crossings that slice declared.

Both are normative and universal, so under DEC-NODE-TYPE-GRID both are
REQUIREMENTS -- and neither needs a node type to carry it.

PROPOSED, and not ruled: SLICE does not survive as a type either. It is WORK
that DECLARES ITS CROSSINGS, and the Crosses edge IS the lane. Slice-ness then
becomes derived -- work that crosses more than one layer -- rather than
announced by a prefix, which is the same move the grid made everywhere else:
prefer a property read off the edges to a type asserted up front.

What that buys is a rule with somewhere to live. "Work does not touch what it
did not declare" is checkable: the changes a piece of work lands should fall
inside what it declared it crosses. That is an L3 rule sitting on L2 hooks, not
a concept to be remembered, and it cannot be written at all while the discipline
exists only as a name.

NO FORCING FUNCTION TODAY. Nothing checks lane discipline, and nothing will
until L3 has somewhere to put the rule, so leaving this fuzzy costs nothing in
the meantime. It is recorded rather than decided so that the concept is not lost
between now and then.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The operator recalled on 2026-08-28 that SLICE exists as a scope-discipline device -- stay in your horizontal layer unless you are a slice, and do not veer outside your slice when implementing across layers -- and was unsure whether that warrants a node. Written down because a discipline carried only as a name is one session away from being forgotten, and because naming it as normative gives the migration a pre-classified node rather than another to test by hand.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
NORMATIVE, pre-classified. When the grid's vocabulary lands this becomes a
REQUIREMENT, not a MECHANISM; it is written as a mechanism only because
REQUIREMENT is not in the grammar yet.

RULED 2026-08-28, shortly after being written. The proposal this node carried
was accepted as DEC-SLICE-DECOMPOSES-TO-WORK, so the alternative it recorded as
still live -- leaving the discipline a fuzzy concept until a forcing function
appeared -- is no longer. What remains here is the pair of rules themselves,
awaiting the vocabulary that can express them.
<<<
